                    Part I — Additional Conditions

1. ContentDesk may be used commercially, subject to the following conditions:

   a. Hosted and embedded commercial use: Unless You have obtained a written
      commercial license from the Producer, You may not use ContentDesk, in
      Source or Object form and in whole or substantial part, to:

      - provide a hosted or managed ContentDesk service to third parties;
      - operate a public ContentDesk instance for users outside Your own
        organization, whether the service is paid, free, advertising-supported,
        sponsored, or otherwise monetized;
      - embed ContentDesk as a component of a product or service that is sold,
        licensed, resold, or otherwise commercially distributed to third
        parties; or
      - provide ContentDesk through an OEM, white-label, managed service
        provider, or commercial platform offering.

      Internal use within a single organization, including multiple workspaces
      and deployment on infrastructure controlled by that organization, does
      not require a commercial license. Personal use, research, evaluation,
      testing, and modifications solely for such permitted use also do not
      require a commercial license.

      Publishing or distributing the Source form of a fork is not itself a
      hosted service and does not require a commercial license. Any such
      distribution remains subject to this complete ContentDesk License, and
      each operator must independently comply with this condition.

      A consultant may charge for installing, configuring, or supporting
      ContentDesk on infrastructure controlled by the consultant's customer.
      This exception does not permit the consultant to operate ContentDesk as a
      hosted or multi-customer service without a commercial license.

      An independent plugin, template, theme, connector, or external tool that
      interoperates through ContentDesk's public APIs, MCP interfaces, file
      formats, or Plugin SDK may be sold commercially without a commercial
      license, provided that it does not copy or embed a substantial part of
      the ContentDesk Source form.

   b. Branding: Unless the Producer has granted You a written branding waiver,
      You may not remove or modify the ContentDesk logo, the ContentDesk product
      name, user-interface copyright, license or attribution notices, or any
      "Powered by ContentDesk" notice included in a ContentDesk user interface.

      For purposes of this condition, a "ContentDesk user interface" means any
      user interface derived, in whole or substantial part, from user-interface
      code in this repository, including code located in `apps/desktop/`,
      `apps/web/`, `apps/native/`, `apps/mobile/`, `packages/ui/`,
      `packages/views/`, `packages/ui-web/`, `packages/ui-native/`,
      `packages/editor/`, `packages/canvas/`, or `packages/timeline/`, and any
      desktop, web, or mobile application built from that code. This list is
      illustrative rather than exhaustive. The condition continues to apply if
      covered code is modified, moved, renamed, split into other packages, or
      transferred to another repository.

      This condition does not apply to uses that do not involve a ContentDesk
      user interface. Such uses remain subject to condition 1(c).

   c. Attribution without a user interface: If You redistribute or operate a
      product or service built on ContentDesk's Server, Local Engine, Worker,
      CLI, MCP capabilities, or content-processing modules without a
      ContentDesk user interface, You must:

      - retain all copyright, patent, trademark, license, and attribution
        notices present in the Source form, LICENSE, and NOTICE;
      - state in user-facing documentation that the product or service is built
        on ContentDesk;
      - include a link to the Official Repository; and
      - not state or imply that the product or service is an official
        ContentDesk distribution.

   d. Independent grants: A branding waiver is not a commercial license and
      does not permit any use restricted by condition 1(a). A commercial
      license does not itself waive condition 1(b) or 1(c). Each grant must be
      obtained expressly and separately from the Producer in writing. Neither
      grant may be inferred from the other grant or from the Producer's silence,
      code review, or acceptance of a Contribution.

